# CLI & TypeScript Conventions

## CLI design

- Commands should be composable and predictable (`init`, `scaffold`, `serve`, `mcp`, `eval`, `doctor`, etc.)
- Support both interactive and non-interactive / CI-friendly usage
- Prefer explicit flags over hidden behavior
- Include `--dry-run` for any command that mutates the workspace, writes files, or talks to external systems
- Help text should be concise and show the happy path first
- Prefer idempotent behavior where practical: re-running scaffold should not thrash careful user edits without intent

## TypeScript

- Strict mode preferred
- Prefer interfaces / types that model the domain clearly (ToolDefinition, Resource, Prompt, Schema, ScaffoldResult, EvalCase, etc.)
- Avoid deep inheritance; favor composition
- Keep modules focused — one primary responsibility per file when practical
- Public CLI and MCP tool surface should be stable; internal modules can evolve faster
- Prefer shared types between server tools and any client/scaffold output so contracts stay aligned

## Error handling

- Fail fast with clear messages
- Tell the user the next concrete action when possible
- Distinguish between user error (bad schema, missing config) and system error
- Never swallow tool or transport errors silently

## Testing mindset

- Core tool handlers, schema validation, and scaffold transforms should be unit-testable
- Prefer pure functions for schema → artifact transformation where possible
- CLI integration tests can come later; focus on domain logic and tool contracts first
- Eval cases are part of the quality bar, not an afterthought
